    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>House hacking means buying a home you live in and renting out part of it, such as the other side of a duplex, a garage apartment or spare bedrooms, so tenants cover some or all of your mortgage. Because you live there, you can use owner-occupied financing with a small down payment, which makes house hacking one of the most accessible ways to start investing in Texas real estate.</p>
<p>It is not free money. Done right, though, it lowers your housing cost, builds equity and leaves you with a rental when you move out.</p>

<h2>How house hacking works</h2>
<p>The core idea is simple: loans for homes you occupy have lower down payments and usually better rates than investment property loans. A two- to four-unit property still counts as owner-occupied if you live in one unit. You get investor-style income with homebuyer-style financing.</p>
<p>The common versions are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Small multifamily:</strong> buy a duplex, triplex or fourplex and live in one unit.</li>
<li><strong>Accessory dwelling unit (ADU):</strong> buy a house with a garage apartment or back cottage, or build one, and rent it out.</li>
<li><strong>Renting rooms:</strong> buy a single-family home with extra bedrooms and rent them to roommates.</li>
<li><strong>Live-in, then rent:</strong> live in the home for the required period, move out and rent the whole property.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Financing a house hack: FHA, conventional and VA</h2>
<h3>FHA: 3.5% down on 1-4 units</h3>
<p>FHA loans allow a minimum down payment of 3.5% with a credit score of 580 or higher, on properties with up to four units, as long as you occupy one as your primary residence. FHA charges an upfront mortgage insurance premium of 1.75% of the base loan (usually financed) plus an annual premium, commonly 0.55% for a 30-year loan with minimum down. With less than 10% down, that annual premium generally lasts for the life of the loan unless you refinance.</p>
<p>FHA loan limits are higher for multi-unit properties and vary by county. Your lender can tell you the current limit for Harris, Fort Bend, Montgomery or wherever you are buying.</p>
<h3>The self-sufficiency test for 3-4 units</h3>
<p>Triplexes and fourplexes face an extra hurdle. Under FHA's self-sufficiency test, lenders generally take 75% of the appraiser's estimate of fair market rent for all units, including yours, and that figure must cover the full monthly payment including taxes and insurance. With Texas property taxes, many fourplexes fail. Duplexes are not subject to this test, which is one reason duplexes are the most popular house hack.</p>
<h3>Conventional: 5% down on 2-4 units</h3>
<p>Since late 2023, Fannie Mae has allowed as little as 5% down on owner-occupied two- to four-unit homes, down from the much larger down payments it required before. Conventional loans have private mortgage insurance that can be removed once you build enough equity, which can make them cheaper over time than FHA. Reserve requirements can be significant, so ask your lender.</p>
<h3>VA loans</h3>
<p>Eligible veterans and service members can use a VA loan with no down payment on a property of up to four units if they occupy one. If you qualify, it is worth comparing.</p>
<p>Lenders commonly count a portion of projected rent from the other units, often 75%, toward your qualifying income. Ask how your lender treats it before you shop.</p>

[[tool:mortgage-calculator]]

<h2>ADUs and renting rooms in Houston</h2>
<p>Houston has no conventional zoning, and the city's development code allows secondary dwelling units such as garage apartments on many single-family lots. That makes ADUs a realistic house hacking path here, especially in older inner-loop neighborhoods where garage apartments are common.</p>
<p>The real constraint is usually private, not public. Deed restrictions and HOA covenants can prohibit a second dwelling, limit occupancy, ban short-term rentals or require architectural approval. In Houston, deed restrictions are taken seriously and can be enforced. Before you buy or build, read the recorded restrictions and any HOA rules, and check permitting requirements with the city. Suburban cities such as Sugar Land, Pearland or Katy have their own zoning and ADU rules.</p>
<p>Renting rooms is the easiest house hack to start, since you can do it in almost any single-family home. You trade more privacy for the lowest barrier to entry. Use a written lease for each tenant, run consistent screening and follow fair housing law.</p>

[[product:schlage-encode-lock]]

<h2>Texas-specific details: homestead, taxes and insurance</h2>
<h3>Homestead exemption on your unit only</h3>
<p>Texas lets you claim a residence homestead exemption on the home you live in as your principal residence. On a duplex or fourplex, the exemption applies to the unit you occupy, not the rented units. After voters approved Proposition 13 in November 2025, the school district homestead exemption is $140,000 of appraised value, and many counties and cities offer additional optional exemptions. Apply through your appraisal district and ask how it splits value on a multi-unit property. Our <a href="/blog/texas-homestead-exemption/">Texas homestead exemption guide</a> covers deadlines and paperwork, and the <a href="https://comptroller.texas.gov/taxes/property-tax/exemptions/">Texas Comptroller's exemptions page</a> lists the rules.</p>
<h3>Taxes and insurance still dominate the budget</h3>
<p>Even with the exemption, the rented portion is taxed at full value. Combined rates in much of the Houston area run roughly 2% to 3% of appraised value. Insurance for a multi-unit property, including landlord coverage on the rented units, is also expensive in our wind and flood exposure. Get real quotes before you write an offer.</p>
<h3>Income taxes</h3>
<p>Rental income from the other units is taxable, and you can generally deduct expenses and depreciation allocated to the rented portion. The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc414">IRS rental income and expenses page</a> explains the basics. Confirm the details with a CPA, because allocation between personal and rental use matters.</p>

<h2>Worked example: a Houston-area duplex with FHA</h2>
<p>Here is a hypothetical duplex. The numbers are illustrative assumptions, not quotes, so plug in real figures for any property you consider.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Assumption</th><th>Monthly</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Purchase price</td><td>$360,000</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Down payment (3.5%)</td><td>$12,600</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Loan with 1.75% upfront MIP financed</td><td>$353,480</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Principal and interest</td><td>30 years at an assumed 6.5%</td><td>$2,234</td></tr>
<tr><td>Annual FHA MIP</td><td>0.55% of base loan</td><td>$159</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property taxes</td><td>2.2% of price, minus an estimated $1,400 a year in school-tax savings from the homestead exemption on your unit</td><td>$543</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurance</td><td>$3,600 per year</td><td>$300</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total housing payment</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>$3,236</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Rent from other unit</td><td>$1,500</td><td>-$1,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reserve for vacancy and repairs</td><td>15% of rent</td><td>+$225</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Your net housing cost</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>$1,961</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>How to read this</h3>
<p>Your out-of-pocket cost is about $1,961 a month instead of $3,236. If a comparable rental would cost you about $1,500, house hacking costs more per month in this scenario, not less. What you get for that difference is ownership: roughly $3,950 of principal paid down in year one, any appreciation, and a property that keeps producing income.</p>
<p>The bigger payoff often comes after you move out. Rent both units at $1,500 and gross income doubles to $3,000 a month, though the homestead exemption ends and your taxes rise, so rerun the numbers with our <a href="/tools/rental-property-calculator/">rental property calculator</a> before you rely on that outcome.</p>
<p>At higher interest rates, very few house hacks let you live entirely free. Treat that as a bonus, not the plan.</p>

<h2>Choosing the right property for a house hack</h2>
<p>The best house hack is a property you would be comfortable living in and that still works as a full rental after you leave. Judge it on both counts.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Layout:</strong> separate entrances, separate utility meters and some sound separation make life easier for you and your tenants. Shared meters mean you either absorb utilities or build them into rent.</li>
<li><strong>Location:</strong> pick an area where tenants want to live, near job centers such as the Medical Center, Downtown, the Energy Corridor or major universities, with reasonable commutes.</li>
<li><strong>Condition:</strong> a modest cosmetic project can add value, but a first house hack is not the place for a major structural rehab on an FHA loan.</li>
<li><strong>Flood risk:</strong> check the flood zone and the property's flood history, and price flood insurance before you commit.</li>
<li><strong>Exit plan:</strong> make sure the property still cash flows, or at least breaks even, once you pay rent elsewhere and lose the homestead exemption.</li>
</ul>
<p>Duplexes are relatively scarce in many Houston suburbs and more common in older neighborhoods closer to the urban core, so be ready to compromise on location or broaden your search.</p>

<h2>Risks and how to manage them</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Living next to your tenants.</strong> Boundaries matter. Handle requests in writing and keep the relationship professional.</li>
<li><strong>Vacancy hits harder.</strong> With one rental unit, a vacant month means you cover the whole payment. Keep at least several months of payments in reserve.</li>
<li><strong>Older multifamily stock.</strong> Many Houston duplexes are decades old. Inspect the foundation, roof, plumbing, electrical and each HVAC system.</li>
<li><strong>Restrictions.</strong> Deed restrictions or HOA rules can block an ADU, roommates or short-term rentals.</li>
<li><strong>Occupancy rules.</strong> Owner-occupied loans require you to actually live there, generally for at least a year. Do not stretch this.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>House hacking lets Texas buyers use 3.5% to 5% down owner-occupied financing to buy a duplex, fourplex or ADU property and have tenants share the cost. Model taxes, insurance and vacancy honestly, run the FHA self-sufficiency test on three- and four-unit deals, read the deed restrictions, and claim your homestead exemption on your unit. Talk to a lender early, then learn <a href="/blog/how-to-analyze-a-rental-property/">how to analyze a rental property</a> before you make an offer.</p>
